    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `format_for_display` in `app/report.py`, and all 3 tests pass (the 2 that were already there plus 1 new one).\n\n**How it works:** it uses `DataFrame.map` to run a small helper, `_format_amount`, on every cell. The result is a new table with the same index and columns, and the input table is left unchanged.\n- Missing values become `\"\"`.\n- Each amount is rounded to two decimals. Anything that rounds to zero, like `-0.001`, becomes `\"-\"` so you never see `\"(0.00)\"`.\n- Other amounts get thousands separators and two decimals, and negatives go in parentheses: `12500` becomes `\"12,500.00\"` and `-634.2` becomes `\"(634.20)\"`.\n- The result's column type is set to plain Python strings, because pandas 3 might otherwise pick its own string type.\n\nI added `tests/test_report.py`, which checks each of those cases, a large number (`1,234,567.89`), that the index and columns match, that every cell is a string, and that the input table is unchanged.\n\nI couldn't look up the pandas 3 docs because Context7 returned \"monthly quota exceeded\". The passing tests confirm that `DataFrame.map` works in the installed pandas 3.0.6. No dependencies were added."
  },
